\addvspace {10\p@ }
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2.1}{\ignorespaces Simplified event log with two truck visits. Case attributes such as the number of planned stops are stored once per case and are not shown.}}{8}{table.caption.10}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2.2}{\ignorespaces Research directions and the questions they leave open for the CTB case study.}}{25}{table.caption.14}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces Operational data sources.}}{28}{table.caption.15}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3.2}{\ignorespaces From the truck schedule to the event log. A visit is counted under its first reason; visits with a missing timestamp and a turnaround over twelve hours are counted as missing timestamp.}}{29}{table.caption.17}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3.3}{\ignorespaces Case attributes of the simulation: the planned visit, known at Gate In.}}{30}{table.caption.18}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3.4}{\ignorespaces Visit structure and turnaround time (TTT) of the event log. Approach: Gate In to the start of the first stop; exit: end of the last stop to Gate Out; transfer: time between stops. Gate events are instantaneous, so the four parts add up to the TTT. Delay-peak hours: Gate-In hours with at least 20 visits and a median TTT of at least 37 minutes, the 90th percentile of the training hours.}}{32}{table.caption.19}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3.5}{\ignorespaces Domain rules for truck visits. The type decides where a rule can be enforced (Section~\ref {sec:exp_rules}).}}{33}{table.caption.20}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces Discovery configurations of E1, all run in ProM Lite 1.4 on the training log (59,139 visits). The iDHM frequency threshold is relative to the number of visits: at 0.1, only directly-follows relations that occur in at least a tenth of the visits are used. Because no data attributes were selected, iDHM discovers no conditional dependencies, and the conditional heuristic (C4.5 with F1 score, condition threshold 0.5) has no effect.}}{42}{table.caption.24}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces What a coverage threshold leaves out of the required behaviour (training log, 59,139 visits, 82 sequences). A sequence below the threshold is not required. Mean TTT of all visits: 37 minutes. P90: 64 minutes. Multi-block visits: visits to two or more RMG blocks.}}{43}{table.caption.25}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.3}{\ignorespaces Measures of E3. The capacity of the blocks depends on the simulator, not on the net, and is examined in E4.}}{46}{table.caption.26}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.4}{\ignorespaces Measures of operational validity in E4. For each load profile in P4, the \emph {profile gap} is the mean absolute difference of the simulated medians to the observed ones over the bins.}}{48}{table.caption.27}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.5}{\ignorespaces Mechanisms examined in E4. Observed values from the test log; the training log gives the same profiles within one to two minutes.}}{50}{table.caption.28}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.6}{\ignorespaces Configurations of E4. ProSiT: component as fitted in E3. No self-count: retrained without counting the stop in its own queue. Lane free: waiting measured from the moment a lane became free. Whole delay: waiting measured from enabling to start. Every configuration from b3 on, and the baseline a0, is also run with an exact count of the activities in progress (suffix x, see the design decisions).}}{56}{table.caption.29}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.7}{\ignorespaces Threats to the conclusions and the design responses.}}{57}{table.caption.30}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5.1}{\ignorespaces Components of the simulation model. Arrivals and case attributes are used only when the model generates its own trucks (Section~\ref {sec:sim_modes}).}}{60}{table.caption.32}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5.2}{\ignorespaces Capacities and calendars. The discovered resource calendars cover 122 to 128 of the 168 hours of a week; they are set to the full week so that trucks already inside are served to completion. The gate calendar still controls admission.}}{62}{table.caption.36}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5.3}{\ignorespaces Discovery settings of the simulation model.}}{63}{table.caption.37}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5.4}{\ignorespaces Extensions of the simulation model compared with ProSiT 1.0.4.}}{64}{table.caption.38}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5.5}{\ignorespaces One simulated truck in replay mode (configuration selected in E4, seed 42), step by step. The leaf of each tree follows from the features at that moment; the waiting and execution times are drawn from the distribution of that leaf; the waiting time includes any delay until a lane is free (capacity checked over the whole stop at its start).}}{66}{table.caption.39}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.1}{\ignorespaces E1 in two levels for the sound nets, training log (59,139 visits, 82 sequences). Level 1: share of fitting visits and escaping-edges precision (ProM 6.15, Section~\ref {sec:exp_discovery_eval}); Gen.: new test sequences accepted, of 16. Level 2: training sequences the net rejects, above the coverage threshold (of 17) and below it (of 65), and the share of the truck-minutes (sum of the turnaround times), of the visits above the training P90 of 64 minutes and of the multi-block visits that the rejected visits make up. Coverage check: accepts every sequence above the coverage threshold. \textsuperscript {a}\,ETConformance rejects 30 fitting visits of IM 0.0 (Section~\ref {sec:res_e1_level1}). The prefix tree is a reference, not a candidate.}}{68}{table.caption.40}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.2}{\ignorespaces Rule audit of the three nets that pass the soundness and coverage checks, as discovered. Rule 3: a visit without a stop is in the language (shortest witness \texttt {Gate In > Gate Out}). Rule 4: a reachable marking enables two stops at the same time. Rule 5: a mixed stop together with a separate delivery or receive at the same LL or HO2 area is in the language. Bound of rule 6: loops permit any number of stops; the stop guard enforces the planned number in the simulator. A3: rules 3 to 5 hold. Prec.: ETConformance (training).}}{71}{table.caption.43}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.3}{\ignorespaces E3, simulated visits against the training log, means over five seeds. Overlap: share of multi-stop visits with overlapping stops. Two RMG blocks: share of the visits with an RMG stop that use two blocks. Unseen: generated visits whose sequence does not occur in training. Guard, per run: draws whose weights the guard changed; draws in which every permitted step had a learned weight of zero, so that the guard chose among the permitted steps; draws it could not decide.}}{72}{table.caption.44}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.4}{\ignorespaces Number of stops per visit: training log against the simulation of HM 0.7 (E3, means over five seeds of 32,014 visits). With the stop guard every truck performs its planned number of stops; without it, the learned routing alone reproduces the distribution almost as closely.}}{72}{table.caption.45}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.5}{\ignorespaces ProSiT's waiting-time target at the RMG blocks by the load of the block when the stop is enabled, training log. The target is measured from the moment the block was last completely free (single server).}}{76}{table.caption.46}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.6}{\ignorespaces Error attribution along the path of the truck (E4a--E4d), against the test log, means over five seeds. From b3x on, the observed demand and the observed blocks are used, so that only the queue and the service vary. The rows in italics are differences between real periods. Med., P90: median and P90 of the TTT; CTD: cycle-time distance; Appr., Stops: mean approach and stop time per visit (all in minutes). Top bl.: share of the RMG stops at the busiest block in \%. Max: largest number of trucks at one block at once in any seed. Spear.: Spearman correlation of hourly arrivals and hourly median TTT. G-appr., G-stop, G-WIP: profile gaps for the approach by trucks at the block, the RMG stop duration by other trucks at the block and the TTT by trucks in the terminal (minutes).}}{77}{table.caption.47}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.7}{\ignorespaces Error of the mean turnaround time (simulated minus observed, minutes) and its parts; the four parts add up to the TTT error up to rounding. The stop error is split into a count part (stops per visit by area, i.e.\ routing) and a duration part (mean stop duration by area). Last row: training log minus test log.}}{80}{table.caption.50}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.8}{\ignorespaces Acceptance criteria of Section~\ref {sec:exp_e4_runs} for the final configuration. Thresholds from the differences between real periods: Spearman of the weakest test week; profile gaps (approach / stop duration / TTT by trucks in the terminal, minutes) of the largest difference between weeks; CTD within the differences between weeks (3.1 minutes) or between periods (5.5 minutes).}}{82}{table.caption.51}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.9}{\ignorespaces Effect of S1 on the turnaround corridor of 25 to 35 minutes: baseline value of the simulation and paired change (S1 minus baseline), mean over ten seeds with 95\,\% $t$-interval. Robustness runs as in Table~\ref {tab:rq3_robustness}, each against its own baseline. Values of the model, not estimates for the terminal.}}{84}{table.caption.52}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.10}{\ignorespaces Scenario S1 against the baseline (final configuration of E4, replayed test period), mean over ten paired seeds with 95\,\% $t$-interval. Values of the model under the conditions of E4, not estimates for the terminal.}}{85}{table.caption.53}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6.11}{\ignorespaces Robustness of S1 $\pm 2$ (Z5): each run against its own baseline, mean over ten paired seeds with 95\,\% $t$-interval. (a) execution time without load; (b) the service model sees one more truck whenever another truck is at the block; (c) arrivals compressed to intensity 1.2; (d) hour-of-week arrivals and training block shares; in (d) the affected visits are compared as groups, not per visit.}}{86}{table.caption.54}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {A.1}{\ignorespaces Turnaround-time components in and outside peak hours. Visits are assigned to the hour of their Gate In.}}{106}{table.caption.59}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {A.2}{\ignorespaces Visits below, in and above the turnaround-time corridor of 25 to 35 minutes, observed logs. Minutes above 35 per visit: mean of $\max (\mathrm {TTT}-35, 0)$ over all visits. The four parts of the turnaround time add up to it. Visits longer than twelve hours were removed as recording errors in the data preparation (Chapter~\ref {chp:case_study_and_data}).}}{108}{table.caption.60}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {B.1}{\ignorespaces Conformance of all discovered nets on the training log (2 March to 11 April 2026) and the test log (13 to 30 April 2026). Fitness, fitting visits and accepted sequences: alignment replay in ProM 6.15 (costs 1 for a move on the log or on a visible transition, 0 for a silent move); fitness is the ProM trace fitness averaged over the visits. Fitting visits: share of visits whose complete sequence the net accepts. Precision: escaping-edges precision, ETConformance in ProM 6.15 on the discovered net, same settings for every net (``Lazy Invisibles'', ``First Indeterminism Solving''). \textsuperscript {a}\,ETConformance counts 30 training and 15 test visits of IM 0.0 as non-fitting that fit under alignments (Section~\ref {sec:exp_discovery_eval}). Seq.\ accepted: training sequences accepted, of 82. Unseen test seq.: test sequences that do not occur in training and are accepted, of 16. Soundness: WOFLAN (PM4Py implementation). Values of unsound nets are diagnostic only. The prefix tree is a reference that accepts exactly the training sequences.}}{110}{table.caption.61}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {B.2}{\ignorespaces Escaping-edges precision of the sound nets of E1 on the training log, by implementation. ProM: ETConformance 6.9.52 with the settings of Section~\ref {sec:exp_discovery_eval}. PM4Py 2.7.22.3: \texttt {precision\_\allowbreak token\_\allowbreak based\_\allowbreak replay} and \texttt {precision\_\allowbreak alignments}. The implementations agree for nets whose silent transitions do not branch; where silent paths branch, PM4Py follows fewer of them and reports a higher value.}}{111}{table.caption.62}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {C.1}{\ignorespaces The three nets that pass the soundness and coverage checks, as discovered and as state machines of the language restricted to rules 3 and 5 (control of Appendix~\ref {app:replica}). Rules violated: rules whose violation the language permits, discovered $\rightarrow $ restricted (6 (bound): more than four stops, enforced by the stop guard). Sequences with 4 stops: distinct sequences the language permits (5 occur in training). Precision: ETConformance in ProM 6.15. Gen.: new test sequences accepted, discovered $\rightarrow $ restricted.}}{113}{table.caption.63}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {C.2}{\ignorespaces Earlier comparison of the three admissible nets, each repaired, rewritten without silent transitions, with the stop guard on the visible transitions; mean over five seeds with [minimum, maximum]. Prec.: ETConformance on the rewritten net (training). Gate: no visit without stop, no overlapping stops of one truck, at most four stops, in every seed. TVD: total-variation distance to the training log (sequences; number of stops per visit). Overr.: guard overrides per run. Unseen: generated visits whose sequence does not occur in the training log. Right: against the test log. Observed test log: TTT median 33, P90 75 minutes.}}{113}{table.caption.64}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {C.3}{\ignorespaces $2\times 2$ contrast for HM 0.7 in E3 (Section~\ref {sec:exp_e3_conditions}): original net (22 places, 45 transitions, 34 silent) or the same language rewritten without silent transitions, stop guard on the visible transitions off or on; mean over five seeds, against the training log. Overlap: share of the multi-stop visits with overlapping stops. Unseen: generated visits whose sequence does not occur in the training log. Guard: draws whose weights the guard changed, and draws it could not decide (overrides), per run.}}{114}{table.caption.65}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {C.4}{\ignorespaces Effect of the rule treatment on the simulated visits (E3), test period, means over five seeds. Original net: IM 0.0 as discovered (parallel branches that silent transitions can skip). Repaired net: language restricted to rules 3 and 5, rewritten without silent transitions. Guard: stop guard on the visible transitions. Control: the unrepaired language rewritten without silent transitions. Overlapping stops: share of the visits with more than one stop. Unseen sequences: generated visits whose sequence never occurs in the training log. TVD: total-variation distance to the distribution of the observed test log (0 = identical).}}{114}{table.caption.66}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {C.5}{\ignorespaces Control-flow replica of ProSiT's routing (token game, no time, no resources): every training visit is simulated with its own case attributes, three seeds, measures against the training log; the sequence TVD of a row varies by at most 0.006 between the seeds. Routing weights: P(transition fires $\mid $ enabled), estimated by counting on the aligned training log; ``data + history'' uses the planned stops, the flow type and the counts of the activities done so far, as ProSiT's trees do; ``marking'' adds the state of the net. Guard: local = the simple guard on visible stops and Gate Out; look-ahead = also masks silent steps after which the plan cannot be completed; rejection = simulate without guard and keep only visits with the planned number of stops. Rewritten: the same language without silent transitions.}}{115}{table.caption.67}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {D.1}{\ignorespaces Choice of the final configuration on the training period (E4f): replayed training visits with their observed blocks, compared with the training log, means over five seeds. Admissible: no rule violation and at most three trucks at a block at once. The configuration with the smallest sum of the profile gaps is chosen ($\leftarrow $). Gaps and CTD in minutes.}}{117}{table.caption.68}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {D.2}{\ignorespaces Final configuration with each change reversed alone, with other block allocations, other arrival intensities and without replay (E4e), against the test log, means over five seeds. $^{*}$Only the 2,393 visits of the first day, because the queue of the learned block choice grows without bound. Med., P90: median and P90 of the TTT; CTD: cycle-time distance; Appr., Stops: mean approach and stop time per visit (all in minutes). Top bl.: share of the RMG stops at the busiest block in \%. Max: largest number of trucks at one block at once in any seed. Spear.: Spearman correlation of hourly arrivals and hourly median TTT. G-appr., G-stop, G-WIP: profile gaps for the approach by trucks at the block, the RMG stop duration by other trucks at the block and the TTT by trucks in the terminal (minutes).}}{118}{table.caption.69}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {D.3}{\ignorespaces The final configuration with each net of the E3 contrasts, against the test log, means over five seeds. Times in minutes. Overlap: share of multi-stop visits with overlapping stops. TTT at 150+: median TTT of the visits with 150 or more trucks in the terminal at Gate In.}}{119}{table.caption.70}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {D.4}{\ignorespaces Key values of the final configuration against the observed logs (replayed visits and observed blocks, means over five seeds). Times in minutes; CTD: cycle-time distance; Spearman: hourly arrivals against hourly median TTT; Max at block: largest number of trucks at one block at once (the log records 4 because of short recording overlaps, Section~\ref {sec:exp_e4_mechanisms}).}}{119}{table.caption.71}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {D.5}{\ignorespaces Load-response profiles against the test log: medians in minutes, means over five seeds. --: fewer than 50 cases in the bin. With three lanes, the bins ``4+ trucks at the block''{} and ``3+ other trucks''{} cannot occur; in the log they are short recording overlaps.}}{121}{table.caption.74}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {D.6}{\ignorespaces Who gains and who loses under S1: paired change of the TTT per visit (the same observed visit in the baseline and the scenario), mean over ten seeds with 95\,\% $t$-interval. A visit belongs to a block group if its first RMG stop starts within 60 minutes of a move away from or to that block.}}{122}{table.caption.75}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {E.1}{\ignorespaces Components of a ProSiT simulation model (version 1.0.4). Times are counted in open minutes of the relevant calendar.}}{124}{table.caption.76}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {E.2}{\ignorespaces Learned components of the simulation model (ProSiT 1.0.4 on HM 0.7 as discovered, training log). Waiting and execution time are the retrained components of the final configuration (Section~\ref {sec:exp_e4_mechanisms}); the inventory of every model is in Appendix~\ref {app:prosit_inventory}.}}{125}{table.caption.77}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {E.3}{\ignorespaces Every learned model of the final simulation model. Leaves: number of leaves; range: smallest and largest mean of the values a leaf gives the simulator (minutes), or firing probability (routing). Every leaf with its distribution, parameters and sample size is listed in \texttt {model\_leaves.csv} of the reproduction package.}}{125}{table.E.3}%
